**Satisfying Custom-Ratio Fairness**

In this section, we discuss the second, more general, fairness definition where the user provides the ratios T={τ1, τ2, ..., τk} and requires the returned ε-net S to satisfy |Sci| = τi for every ci ∈ C.

Through the whole subsection, we assume that we have access to the ranges R; in other words, the ranges are explicitly given to us as input. This assumption is valid in most cases; for example, the discrepancy approach to deterministically find an ε-net is based on this assumption [25,44]. Assuming that we have access to the ranges Rand|R| = m, finding an ε-net of the range space (X,R) can be reduced to the hitting set problem. In other words, based on the Definition 1, Nis an ε-net of(X,R), if and only if it hits all the ranges in Rε = {R | R ∈ R, |R| ≥ ε}. Given the range space (X,R), one can first filter out the light ranges and keep only the heavy ranges, aka Rε. Then, compute a hitting set for the new range space (X,Rε). Any algorithm solving the hitting set problem would result in solving the ε-net problem.

ε-net reduction → over Rε Hitting Set

In the fairness context, any fair hitting set given the constraints T is also a fair ε-net. Solving the Fair Hitting Set problem is discussed in [31] in the general setting. They provide a randomized O(logn)-approximation algorithm for the fair set cover that runs in O(poly(n,m)) time. The approximation factor holds in expectation. The algorithm in [31] is a greedy algorithm that picks the most promising points at each step. As a result, by applying the same algorithm, we can compute a fair ε-net for the range space (X,R) with expected size O(OPT · logn), where OPT is the fair ε-net with the minimum size. We note that this algorithm does not find a fair ε-net with an absolute size as we had in Theorems 6, 13, and 17. Instead, our algorithm for custom-ratios returns a fair ε-net with size larger by an (expected) O(logn) factor from the smallest possible fair ε-net with respect to the CR constraints T.

Theorem 18. Given a range space (X,R), with |X| = n, |R| = m, CR constraints T, and a parameter ε ∈ (0,1), there exists a randomized algorithm that constructs a fair ε-net with respect to T, of expected size O(OPT · logn) in O(poly(n,m)) time, where OPT is the size of the smallest fair ε-net with respect to T.

The algorithm from Theorem 18 works in general range spaces; however, assuming a range space (X,R) with a bounded VC-dim Fair Geometric Hitting Set (FGHS) problem is discussed in detail in §5. Here, we only use the final result we get for the FGHS problem and use it to derive an approximation algorithm for constructing a fair ε-net satisfying CR constraints T. In Theorem 22, we show an O(max{log1τ, dlog(OPTFGHS)} · log(kτ))-approximation algorithm (with probability at least 1−τ) for the Fair Geometric Hitting Set problem (on CR constraints), where OPT FGHS is the optimum solution. By leveraging the algorithm from Theorem 22 on (X,Rε), we return a fair ε-net S with respect to CR constraints T of size O(max{log1τ, dlog(OPTFGHS)} · log(kτ) · OPT) with probability at least 1−τ. The algorithm's time complexity is O(n·m + M(n,m+k)), where M(n,m+k) is the running time to solve a linear program with O(n) variables and O(m+k) constraints (Theorem 22).

Theorem 19. Given a range space (X,R), with |X| = n, |R| = m, CR constraints T, and a parameter ε ∈(0,1), there exists a randomized algorithm that constructs a fair ε-net with respect to T, of size O(max{log1τ, dlog(OPT)} · log(kτ) · OPT) with probability at least 1−τ, in O(n·m + M(n,m+k)) time, where OPT is the size of the smallest fair ε-net with respect to T, k is the number of colors in X, and M(n,m+k) is the running time to solve a linear program with O(n) variables and O(m+k) constraints.

It is worth mentioning that in §5, the Fair Geometric Hitting Set is solved by a reduction to the weighted fair ε-net with DP constraints. As a result, the whole algorithm reduces an instance of the fair ε-net with CR constraint to an instance of the weighted fair ε-net problem with DP constraints.

Fairε-net (CR)red.−→ FGHS (CR)red.−→ Weighted Fair ε-net (DP)

CR constraints. In this example, any ε-sample must contain 1/2 ± ε fraction of red points. The reason is that there exists a range R∗ ∈ R that separates all reds from all blue points, and we have |Xred|/|X|=1/2.

